    "body": "U.S. Customs and Border Protection officers discovered nearly $30.4 million in suspected methamphetamine inside a commercial salt shipment in Texas. The October 12 seizure occurred at Laredo’s World Trade Bridge along the U.S.-Mexico border. Officers recovered 3,395.91 pounds of suspected methamphetamine from a 2020 International LT625 tractor-trailer. Authorities subsequently released the driver and equipment while federal investigators continued examining the case.\nOfficers discovered 76 bags containing suspected methamphetamine inside a trailer manifested as salt. The agency referred the investigation to Homeland Security Investigations (HSI). Officials have not publicly established whether the driver or trucking company knew about the concealed drugs.\nCommercial shipment leads to multimillion-dollar seizure\nA CBP officer selected the commercial vehicle for additional examination during the crossing. Officers conducted a secondary inspection using nonintrusive imaging technology before uncovering the concealed narcotics.\nAn HSI officer examines bags associated with the methamphetamine seizure at Laredo’s World Trade Bridge. (Photos courtesy of U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement)\nLaredo is the nation’s busiest commercial land port, handling 2.95 million inbound truck crossings from Mexico in 2025, according to federal transportation data . In September, federal investigators uncovered more than 2,000 pounds of methamphetamine concealed inside a commercial cabbage shipment in South Texas.\nDriver and tractor-trailer released\nFreightWaves asked why authorities released the driver and equipment. Questions also included the carrier’s identity and country of registration, importer of record and shipment destination. CBP and HSI declined to provide those details, citing the ongoing investigation.\nIn a statement to FreightWaves, HSI outlined its ongoing investigation. “HSI continues to investigate the matter and pursue all available leads to identify those responsible for bringing this significant quantity of methamphetamine into the United States and prevent it from reaching communities across South Texas,” the agency stated. FreightWaves will update this article as new information becomes available.\nWhy it matters\nCommercial freight shipments can conceal substantial quantities of narcotics, placing trucking companies and drivers within federal investigations. The driver’s reported release makes the circumstances surrounding this particular shipment especially relevant to transportation professionals.\nClick here for more articles on cargo theft and freight fraud by Phil Brink.\nCalifornia trucking owner pleads guilty in $2.1M identity theft scheme – FreightWaves\n25 charged after drug ring used unsuspecting truckers to haul cocaine from California to Florida – FreightWaves\nNebraska deputies recover $250K stolen copper load 50 miles past Iowa destination – FreightWaves\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nCompliance\nBrokerage Compliance Symposium\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nAwards\nF3 Awards Dinner\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nCompliance Brokerage Compliance Symposium Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nAwards F3 Awards Dinner Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nFreightTech F3: Future of Freight Festival Oct 27 – Oct 28 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now",
